# jq — was documented as tier=direct, but that was never actually verified:
# jq is seeded/held on most desktops already, so `verify jq --version` was
# silently passing via the *system* jq on PATH, never the relocated copy.
# Found by testing a genuinely fresh, unseeded container (jq isn't part of a
# minimal Debian base): the relocated $PREFIX/usr/bin/jq fails outright —
# `libjq.so.1: cannot open shared object file` — it's a plain DT_NEEDED
# lookup via the default linker search path, not RPATH, so relocating
# breaks it exactly like any other baked-in-path case. LD_LIBRARY_PATH
# fixes it; confirmed against the actual relocated binary, not a fallback.
package  jq
install  ok
tier     env
env      LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$PREFIX/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
verify   jq --version
note     arch-pinned LD_LIBRARY_PATH; regenerate the triplet per host (same caveat as pmarkdown.recipe)
